# NAC3 contract API -- static, public, read-only.
#
# This subtree is an API in the only sense that matters to an agent:
# stable URLs that return data. There is no server-side code here and
# that is deliberate -- nothing to inject into, nothing to take over,
# and the published bytes are hash-verifiable from outside (see
# scripts/verify-contract-api.mjs).
#
# CORS is not a nicety here. The consumer this exists for is an agent
# running inside a page on somebody else's origin; without the header
# below, the browser refuses to hand it the response body and the
# contract is readable only by curl. Measured on 2026-10-24: the host
# sent no Access-Control-Allow-Origin, so this is the line that makes
# the API actually consumable.
#
# Allow-Origin is * on purpose: the contract is public data, carries
# no credentials, and is served with no cookies. There is nothing here
# to protect from being read -- only from being altered unnoticed, and
# that is the job of the hash chain, not of an origin check.

<FilesMatch "\.json$">
    Header set Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"
    Header set Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, HEAD, OPTIONS"
    Header set Access-Control-Max-Age "600"
    # No sniffing: this is JSON and must be read as JSON.
    Header set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
</FilesMatch>

AddType "application/json; charset=UTF-8" .json

# The index, the latest pointer, the trust anchor and the signature MOVE
# on every release, so they are the files that must not be cached for
# long. Five minutes matches the rest of the site.
#
# trust.json and attestation.json belong in this list and not in the
# frozen one: a stale cached signature reads to a consumer as a host
# serving a signature that no longer covers the index -- that is, as a
# tampered publication. Caching a signature is how you manufacture a
# false alarm.
<FilesMatch "^(index|latest|trust|attestation)\.json$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=300"
</FilesMatch>
